Cell["\<\
On the triangular and the hexagonal tilings the endpoints keep gathering past \
the threshold: the straightening of the most visited member comes first, the \
narrowing of the beam later.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"dd9d0a6d-5552-4fed-9e7f-c04b4e2218a9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On the square tiling the spread never falls. At a large scale the beam \
fills the cone of two lattice directions, and its endpoints lie on one \
antidiagonal, all at the same distance from the germ: the path metric of the \
square lattice is the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["\[ScriptL]", "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "e805c58d-a370-4b65-9779-f9420d2868ce"],
 " metric."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"d8a279f8-e4d3-49ac-92ec-996a56082ca2"],

Cell["Open questions", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d92e3d53-6980-479f-8061-12ac23ae6ca0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "[[ The inextensible geodesic at scale ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "8cfc116b-ef51-430d-a7f7-3bd285f832fa"],
 " as an object: the infinite walks of the window graph from the germ's \
window. Is its most visited member eventually a ray at every scale above ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox[
   StyleBox["r", "TI"], "*"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4973413e-5d00-4cfb-a96c-c0469bde7f46"],
 ", and how does ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox[
   StyleBox["r", "TI"], "*"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "a7c09e2a-67b5-4b36-b966-981b8268f57c"],
 " depend on the length of the germ? ]]"
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"f45905f0-ac98-46e5-9191-099fec20b9de"],

Cell["\<\
[[ The loss of direction on a lattice: the straight members follow the cone, \
not the germ. Does a mesh or a sprinkling, with no preferred directions, \
restore the germ's direction at large scales? ]]\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"e79e9511-fa2e-458f-abc6-19ed609bbe0b"],

